## DESCRIPTION
## Implicit function theorem
## ENDDESCRIPTION

## DBsubject('Calculus - multivariable')
## DBchapter('Differentiation of multivariable functions')
## DBsection('Implicit function theorem')

################################################################

DOCUMENT();

loadMacros("PGstandard.pl", "PGML.pl", "parserRadioButtons.pl");

TEXT(beginproblem());

################################################################
#
#  Setup

$mc1 = RadioButtons(["\( A_1 \neq 0 \)", "\( B_1 \neq 0 \)", "\( C_1 \neq 0 \)", "\( D_1 \neq 0 \)"], "\( C_1 \neq 0 \)");

$mc2 = RadioButtons(["Insert incorrect answer here", "Insert another incorrect answer here", "\( (A_1, B_1, C_1, D_1) \neq (0,0,0,0) \)", "Insert yet another incorrect answer here"], "\( (A_1, B_1, C_1, D_1) \neq (0,0,0,0) \)");

$mc3 = RadioButtons(["\( C_1 D_2 \neq C_2 D_1 \)", "Insert incorrect answer here", "Insert another incorrect answer here", "Insert yet another incorrect answer here"], "\( C_1 D_2 \neq C_2 D_1 \)");

$mc4 = RadioButtons(["Insert incorrect answer here", "\( (A_1, B_1, C_1, D_1)\) and \((A_2, B_2, C_2, D_2) \) are linearly independent", "Insert another incorrect answer here", "Insert yet another incorrect answer here"], "\( (A_1, B_1, C_1, D_1)\) and \((A_2, B_2, C_2, D_2) \) are linearly independent");

################################################################
#
#  Text

BEGIN_PGML


Let [` F: \mathbb{R}^4 \to \mathbb{R} `] and [` G: \mathbb{R}^4 \to \mathbb{R} `] be real-valued maps. Fix a point [` p \in \mathbb{R}^4 `] such that [` F(p) = G(p) = 0 `]. Assume that

>>[` \nabla F(p) = (A_1,B_1,C_1,D_1) \qquad \nabla G(p) = (A_2,B_2,C_2,D_2) `]<<

for fixed constants [` A_1,A_2,B_1,B_2,C_1,C_2,D_1,D_2 \in \mathbb{R} `].

What condition on the constants [` A_1,B_1,C_1,D_1 \in \mathbb{R} `] guarantees that you can locally solve the equation

>>[` F(w,x,y,z) = 0 `]<<

for [` y `] in terms of [` w,x, `] and [` z `]?

[_]{$mc1}

What condition on the constants [` A_1,B_1,C_1,D_1 \in \mathbb{R} `] guarantees that you can locally solve the equation

>>[` F(w,x,y,z) = 0 `]<<

for some choice of 1 variable in terms of the other 3 variables?

[_]{$mc2}

What condition on the constants [` A_1,A_2,B_1,B_2,C_1,C_2,D_1,D_2 \in \mathbb{R} `] guarantees that you can locally solve the system of equations

>>[` F(w,x,y,z) = 0, \qquad G(w,x,y,z) = 0 `]<<

for [` y,z `] in terms of [` w,x `]?

[_]{$mc3}

What condition on the constants [` A_1,A_2,B_1,B_2,C_1,C_2,D_1,D_2 \in \mathbb{R} `] guarantees that you can locally solve the system of equations

>>[` F(w,x,y,z) = 0, \qquad G(w,x,y,z) = 0 `]<<

for some choice of 2 variables in terms of the other 2 variables?

[_]{$mc4}
END_PGML

$showPartialCorrectAnswers = 0;

ENDDOCUMENT();